Opening
“Thanks, everyone. We’ve brought together feedback from the Treasury and CEC validations. I’ll start with our own experience in Validation, then cover the feedback and suggestions for the other teams.

Some experiences differed between projects, so we’ve made those distinctions on the slides.”


What went well
“From our side, we supported progress on Bundesbank and material findings through our reviews and challenge. We identified major model issues early and communicated them.

For the Treasury models, regular discussions helped us explain our concerns, understand the constraints and work towards practical solutions.

We also updated our assessments as further evidence became available. In some cases, that helped us distinguish an issue with the model from an explanation that was missing from the documentation. That involved quite a bit of work on the TVRs as the submissions developed.”

Keep this about Validation’s contribution. Save praise for Development and Treasury Risk for the later slide.

Where Validation can improve
“There are also some things we can improve ourselves.

First, we should use the TDD quality checklist consistently at the start, so that we identify missing information early.

Second, we should allow enough time for peer review. Our reports and findings need to explain the concern clearly and make it easy to understand what needs to be addressed.

For CEC specifically, the lesson is to establish the communication framework earlier. That means agreeing how we discuss questions, follow up on responses and deal with points that are blocked. Communication improved during the project, and it would help to have that arrangement in place from the beginning.”

Keep the CEC label. The preparation discussion did not identify the same communication problem for Treasury models.

Positive feedback for the other teams
“We also want to recognise the work from Development and Treasury Risk.

For the Treasury models, availability, regular communication and effective prioritisation helped the validations progress. We could arrange discussions when needed, and there was a willingness to work through the issues together.

Treasury Risk also provided useful explanations when we needed to understand particular aspects of the models. We appreciated that our feedback was taken seriously.

For CEC, once a good communication framework was in place, progress on addressing findings became faster. That’s a useful experience to carry into future projects.”

Say “once the framework was in place” naturally. Avoid stressing “finally” or “eventually”, which can make the positive point sound like another criticism.

Wishlist for the other teams
“Our suggestions mainly concern making the documentation and the review process easier to work with.

On TDD quality, we’d like the important information in the right sections, with clear explanations and less repetition. Detailed supporting material can go in appendices where that helps.

We’d also appreciate having the supporting calculations, minutes and material emails saved in the agreed project folder. Email discussions are useful. The request is simply to keep the supporting record complete and easy to find.

For Treasury models, clearer expectations in the ToR would help, particularly around use cases, deliverables and responsibilities. Having the relevant stakeholders involved early would also reduce the need to revisit discussions.

For CEC, the requests are to follow the agreed priorities, address the most material findings first, and explain the reasons for methodological choices more fully in the TDD.”
